  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA trough is draped across the State, leading to temperatures\nslightly below normal today. This pattern of greater onshore flow\nand cooler weather also allowed for a deeper marine layer that\ncovered inland valleys of Orange and San Diego Counties earlier\nthis morning. We'll give the monsoon another try later this \nafternoon as some mid-level moisture is seen from this morning's \nsounding. Cumulus building over the mountain ridges into the high \ndesert may be able to produce a few showers or weak thunderstorms,\naccompanied by locally gusty winds.\n\nHigh pressure off to the east will slowly expand across the region\nby Sunday into the middle of next week. This will bring a drying\ntrend on Sunday, with chances for any showers across the \nmountains near 10 percent or less. Temperatures for Sunday will be\nvery similar to those of today. Models continue to show high \npressure expanding and strengthening over our area by Monday to \naround Wednesday or Thursday of next week. Temperatures will \nincrease 2-4 degrees each day away from the coastal regions, \nwhere moderate to locally major heat risk is possible for inland \nvalleys and desert regions. The strengthening weather system will \nalso push the marine layer down further, limiting the marine layer\nto cover areas near the coast each night and morning.\n\nModel ensembles show fair agreement on the high pressure system\nsticking around the Desert Southwest into the latter half of next\nweek. The elongated form of the high will eventually become\namplified over the region later in the week. How much\namplification and exact location of the high is still to be\ndetermined. Depending on it's position, it may be able to entrain\nsome monsoonal moisture back into our region as early as \nThursday, possibly lingering into next weekend, which would\ntrigger showers/storms for the mountains and deserts.",
